To use middleware, you typically call `app.use()` and pass a middleware function. For example, to log each request:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next(); // Pass control to the next middleware
});
```
This middleware logs the HTTP method and URL of each incoming request before moving on.

You can create your own middleware to add custom logic. For example, a middleware to check if a user is authenticated:

```js
function checkAuth(req, res, next) {
  if (req.isAuthenticated()) {
    next(); // User is authenticated, proceed
  } else {
    res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  }
}

app.use('/protected-route', checkAuth);
```
Custom middleware helps modularize and reuse code.

Error-handling middleware is a special type of middleware that has four parameters: `(err, req, res, next)`. It catches errors from previous middleware or routes and allows you to respond gracefully. Example:

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err.stack);
  res.status(500).send('Something broke!');
});
```
Always place error middleware after all other app.use() and routes.
